WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:01.810
- [Instructor] In several other videos,

00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:05.970
we have talked about the ideal gas law,

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:10.300
which tells us that pressure times volume

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.540
is going to be equal to

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:15.580
the number of moles times
the ideal gas constant,

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:18.770
times the temperature, measured in Kelvin.

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:22.320
Now in all of our studies
of the ideal gas law,

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:23.990
we assumed that the gases

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:26.930
that we were dealing with were ideal,

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:28.400
And now we're gonna
think a little bit about

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:31.670
what does it mean to be ideal
and what do real gasses,

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:35.360
how do they vary from actual ideal gases?

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:39.520
Well, in order for us to
assume that a gas is ideal,

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:42.420
we assume that its volume,

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:44.620
the volume that the gas takes up,

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:49.620
volume of gas, is negligible, negligible,

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:54.990
relative to container, to container.

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:57.200
The other thing we assume

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.400
is that the molecules of the gas

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.310
don't interact with each other.

00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:05.673
Molecules don't interact.

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:08.860
Now in the real world,

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:13.310
we know that all molecules
take up some volume,

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:15.130
but it could be a reasonable assumption,

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:17.500
if we're talking about
a really huge container,

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:21.100
and we don't have that high
a density of molecules in it,

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:22.960
it's a reasonable assumption

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:25.270
that the volume of the gas itself,

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:29.610
that the molecules themselves
are small in volume,

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:31.950
collectively, relative to the container,

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:35.650
and it's reasonable in many
circumstances to assume

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:38.280
that the molecules don't interact,

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:41.570
maybe they don't have strong
intermolecular forces,

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:43.560
once again, because they're taking up

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.980
a small portion of the volume.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:48.950
They might even not get close
to each other too often.

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:51.830
And so that's why these
are reasonable assumptions,

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:54.740
and they allow us to say
that PV is equal to nRT,

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:56.430
which is a valuable thing,

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:59.540
a valuable approximation
in most circumstances.

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:03.140
But in the real world, we
do know that in actuality,

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:07.210
that each molecule takes up some volume,

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:09.350
and that if you add up all
the molecules together,

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:11.560
they're of course going
to take up some volume,

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.270
and if there's enough molecules,

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:15.040
or if the container is small enough,

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:17.220
we know that the volume of
the gas relative to container

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:18.830
won't be negligible.

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:21.400
We also know that molecules

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:25.110
will interact with each other
in some way, shape or form.

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:26.790
Two molecules can't occupy

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:28.810
the same space at the same time,

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:31.410
so you definitely have
some repulsive forces,

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:33.141
and you have, you might have some,

00:02:33.141 --> 00:02:35.790
even for fairly inert molecules,

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:39.020
you might have some temporary
dipoles that get formed,

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:42.760
some temporary attraction
or some temporary repulsion.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:44.700
So if you're dealing with a situation

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:46.930
where things are less ideal,

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:48.770
and I'm going to make a character of it,

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:52.350
where the molecules are
taking up a significant volume

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:53.620
relative to the container,

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:56.810
you can't say that the
volume of the molecules

00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:58.990
are negligible relative to the container,

00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:02.130
and we assume that they are
interacting with each other,

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:04.290
they're definitely going
to repulse each other,

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:06.830
they can't occupy the same
space at the same time,

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:08.820
but they might attract
each other at some points,

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:11.200
or repulse each other at other points,

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:13.190
and so in this situation,

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:15.770
where we can't make these assumptions,

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:19.363
we're going to have to
modify the ideal gas law.

